# 7. INDIVIDUAL GARMENT MANAGEMENT

This is the most important module.

Every garment must have its own unique database record.

Never treat multiple garments inside one order as a single tracking item.

Each garment contains:

- Internal garment UUID.
- Order ID.
- Garment name.
- Garment category.
- Garment type.
- Brand.
- Color.
- Size.
- Physical tag number.
- Distinctive features.
- Initial condition.
- Stain description.
- Special handling instructions.
- Current department.
- Current status.
- Registration timestamp.
- Registered by.

Examples:

- Black Zara blazer.
- White wedding dress.
- Blue hotel towel.
- Cream-colored duvet.
- Black formal trousers.

### Physical tag policy

IMPORTANT:

ALMOS currently uses physical tags.

Keep the existing manual tagging process.

Do NOT introduce:
- QR codes.
- Barcodes.
- RFID.
- Mandatory scanning hardware.

The internal garment ID is a digital identifier only.

The physical tag number should be stored in the database for cross-reference.

### Garment identification

Allow administrators to upload several photos:

1. Front view.
2. Back view.
3. Brand or label.
4. Physical tag.
5. Stain details.
6. Special characteristics.

Staff must be able to open photos quickly on mobile devices.

If a physical tag is lost, staff can search by:
- Customer/vendor.
- Order ID.
- Garment type.
- Brand.
- Color.
- Distinctive features.
- Reference photos.

Photo matching is an identification aid, not a guarantee. Similar garments must be verified by staff before processing or handover.

